WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
Let's get into First Principles Daily, Episode 54. For July 29th, 2026. Today we reason from raw materials, not analogy. Routine legal documents costing thousands today have an idiot index in the thousands because their raw material cost is essentially zero. A standard will or simple incorporation filing can run two to four thousand dollars in many US markets. Yet the marginal cost of producing one

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:59.660
additional correct document once the template exists is measured in fractions of a cent. The gap between those two figures is not explained by paper, ink or server time. It arises instead from the way the work is still organized, regulated and priced. That separation between near zero marginal cost and observed price is what First Principles Analysis is built to expose. Most routine legal services continue to be delivered through small practices

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:25.620
or solo attorneys who price by the hour. A client pays for every minute spent reviewing facts, selecting clauses and formatting the document even when 90% of the language is drawn from previously used templates. State bar rules that define the practice of law limit who may perform those steps. So tasks that could be handled by trained paralegals or software remain inside the billable envelope.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:35.120
Fragmented demand also plays a role because each matter arrives as a one-off engagement rather than as part of a high volume standardized flow.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:52.400
The result is that a simple lease or uncontested filing carries an effective price that reflects the carrying cost of the lawyer's office, malpractice insurance, continuing education requirements, and the need to spread fixed overhead across a modest number of matters per month.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:00.760
Clients experience the price as normal because the alternative of handling the matter without council has historically carried its own risks.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:17.960
Transparent fixed fee competitors have also been slowed to scale under existing rules. The baseline cost therefore feels like an unavoidable feature of professional judgment rather than a variable that could be attacked through different division of labor or different production methods.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:27.920
An attorney must still spend time confirming that the client's facts fit the template even when the template itself has been validated in hundreds of prior matters.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:39.180
That confirmation step repeats across every new engagement because there is no shared machine readable data layer that would let the same facts be ingested once and reused.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:51.440
The carrying costs compound because each small practice must maintain its own insurance, trust account compliance, and continuing education credits, none of which scale with volume.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:59.020
When volume stays low, the per document share of those fixed costs stays high, locking the price in place.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:07.860
This is why the current arrangement feels inevitable inside the profession even though the underlying document logic has not changed for decades.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:22.740
If a magic wand could instantly arrange the correct words into a legally sound document without any human time, the raw material cost would be the electricity and storage required to hold and transmit a few kilobytes of text.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:30.920
That figure sits well below one cent per document and is already treated as negligible by any high volume online service.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:39.140
The finished price of several thousand dollars therefore produces an idiot index on the order of hundreds of thousands to one.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:49.440
To arrive at that index start with an observed price around two thousand dollars, divide that price by a raw material floor that is a small fraction of a cent.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:58.360
The quotient lands in the hundreds of thousands which simply means the finished service costs that many times more than the physical inputs alone.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:08.260
The bulk of the gap lives in three places. First, the hour spent by a licensed attorney whose time is valued at three to six hundred dollars per hour.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:15.440
Second, the regulatory friction that prevents lower cost providers from performing the same steps at scale.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:27.300
Third, the absence of standardized machine readable inputs that would allow the same document to be generated once and reused thousands of times with only minor variable data.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:35.800
Each of these layers compounds because an hour of review time is multiplied by the number of matters that cannot share a common workflow.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:44.740
The regulatory barrier keeps volume low enough that automation investments never reach the scale seen in other document heavy industries.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:55.300
The arithmetic is straightforward because an estimate of two thousand dollars divided by a sub cent floor yields an index measured in the hundreds of thousands.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.100
Yet the number itself is less important than the visibility it gives to where the cost actually resides.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:11.740
Imagine a single template for an uncontested divorce filing that has already been reviewed by council and accepted by courts in one jurisdiction.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:21.260
Once that template exists, the only variable inputs are names, dates, asset lists, and a handful of factual checkboxes.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:29.280
Those inputs can be collected through a structured form whose marginal cost is the electricity to run the server for a few seconds.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:41.240
If the regulatory environment allowed that filled form to be treated as presumptively valid, the remaining human step would be a quick exception check rather than a full reconstruction of the document.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:50.460
The difference between that exception check model and the current hourly model is exactly where the hundreds of thousands fold multiplier appears.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:06:03.460
Objections that every case is unique lose force once the data show that the overwhelming majority of routine filings fall within a narrow band of fact patterns already covered by published forms.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.780
A redesign would begin by separating the work into three layers.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:17.940
The legal logic itself can be encoded in decision trees or rule engines once and maintain centrally, removing the need to rebuild it for every client.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:26.580
The data collection that populates the logic can shift to guided questionnaires or direct data feeds that replace most of the intake interview.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:40.640
The final review or certification can be reserved for licensed review only on matters that exceed defined risk thresholds, while lower risk outputs receive automated checks plus optional paralegal sign-off.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:52.820
For these moves to reach the floor, three conditions must hold. The encoded logic must be accepted by courts and regulators as presumptively valid when it follows published forms.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.340
Volume must be high enough for the fixed cost of building and auditing the system to be amortized.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:07.040
Unauthorized factors rules must be clarified so that software plus limited human oversight does not trigger sanctions.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.480
The genuinely hard parts are the boundary cases where facts deviate from the template.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:22.280
There is also the political question of how much protection the current rules are meant to provide versus how much they incidentally preserve an existing pricing model.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:29.780
Fixed fee services that already publish prices in the low hundreds of dollars demonstrate that movement is possible.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:39.800
Scaling them further would require the regulatory and data standard changes above rather than further incremental efficiency inside the hourly model.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:51.420
A concrete next step would be to publish the decision tree for one narrow matter type such as a basic revocable living trust, alongside the exact court forms it produces.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:58.160
Regulators could then evaluate whether the output matches the form that an attorney would have produced by hand.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:09.480
If the match rate is high and the exception rate is low, the system could be granted a safe harbor status that lets non attorney operators run it under defined audit requirements.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:22.880
That single precedent would begin to separate the high variance judgment work from the repeatable logic work, lowering the effective idiot index without removing licensed oversight where it actually adds value.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:38.600
When the dominant cost of a document is the calendar time of a highly credentialed professional rather than any scarce physical input, the opportunity lies in redividing the cognitive work so that judgment is applied only where variance is high.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:46.800
When rules written to protect clients also limit the reuse of correct templates across thousands of nearly identical matters,

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:54.620
the first test of progress is whether those rules can be satisfied by auditable systems instead of repeated human hours.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:02.980
Tomorrow, we will examine another domain where the same separation of logic, data, and certification could change the cost curve.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:13.080
Who will publish the first set of machine-checked forms that regulators treat as equivalent to traditional attorney work? That's first principles daily for today.

